---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Piece from '../../components/Piece.astro';
import LazyMount from '../../components/LazyMount.astro';
import { withBase, REPORT_URL } from '../../lib/paths';
---
<Base
  title="Neuron explorer"
  description="Precomputed results for two planted, harmless backdoors in Qwen3.5-4B: the neurons the audits surfaced, ablation curves, sparsity, monitors and one worked trace."
  og="explorer"
  wide
>
  <h1>Explorer</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">
    Everything here is precomputed from <code>results/</code>; no model runs in your browser.
    Read the <a href={withBase('/')}>post</a> first for what the terms mean, or the
    <a href={REPORT_URL}>technical report</a> for the full record.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    Jump to: <a href="#neurons">neuron browser</a> · <a href="#ablation">ablation playground</a> ·
    <a href="#trace">trace view</a> · <a href="#sparsity">sparsity</a> · <a href="#monitor">monitors</a>
  </p>

  <Piece id="neurons" piece="neurons" title="Neuron browser">
    <p>
      The 100 poles per marked model that M3 selected (top 100 of the pre-registered
      triggered − clean ranking), with their rank in every attribution ranking, detection scores,
      generic-corpus contexts with per-token shading, the upstream units that drive them where
      M3 traced them, and M6's subcircuit data for the ten poles M6 studied. A <em>pole</em> is one
      sign of one neuron's activation. Neuron descriptions written by an LLM are withheld in this
      draft.
    </p>
  </Piece>

  <Piece id="ablation" piece="ablation" title="Ablation playground" fallback="fig4_m5_asr_curves"
    fallbackAlt="Triggered ASR against number of units ablated for both markers">
    <p>
      Attack success rate (ASR, the share of greedy replies that contain the marker) and clean-loss
      cost against the number of units ablated, for both markers and every prompt set, with the
      baselines as toggles. Full-size checks and the M6 driver ablation are drawn as diamonds.
    </p>
  </Piece>

  <Piece id="trace" piece="trace" title="Trace view: the token trigger from input to marker logit">
    <p>
      The worked example from the report (section 10), one step at a time. The chain has a gap:
      nothing traced connects layer 6 to layer 27.
    </p>
  </Piece>

  <Piece id="sparsity" piece="sparsity" title="Sparsity (M1)" fallback="fig1_sparsity"
    fallbackAlt="Loss increase against share of neurons kept">
    <p>
      Loss increase when only a fraction of neurons per layer per token is kept, for top, random
      and bottom selections and both fill modes.
    </p>
  </Piece>

  <Piece id="monitor" piece="monitor" title="Monitors (M5)" fallback="fig6_m5_monitor"
    fallbackAlt="AUROC of neuron monitors and residual-stream baselines">
    <p>
      ROC curves for neuron monitors and residual-stream baselines, and a threshold slider that
      counts alarms on each prompt set and on 500 generic-corpus windows.
    </p>
  </Piece>

  <section class="piece">
    <h2>Not built: "play the auditor"</h2>
    <p>
      The plan's last piece would show readers what the blind auditor saw and let them guess the
      trigger. The blind audit (M4) recovered no trigger and every guessed condition was graded a
      miss, so there is nothing for a reader to reconstruct from its outputs, and the inputs it
      synthesized are not published.
    </p>
  </section>
  <LazyMount />
</Base>
